#### Scenario 1: Breaking Change Detection

**Scenario**: Product team modifies IClaimProcessor interface by adding required parameter.

**Detection**:

```csharp
// Before (Product v2.1)
public interface IClaimProcessor
{
    Task<ClaimResult> ProcessAsync(Claim claim, CancellationToken cancellationToken);
}

// After (Product v2.2) - BREAKING CHANGE
public interface IClaimProcessor
{
    Task<ClaimResult> ProcessAsync(
        Claim claim,
        ProcessingContext context,  // NEW REQUIRED PARAMETER
        CancellationToken cancellationToken);
}

// Contract test detects the change
[Fact]
public void IClaimProcessor_DetectsBreakingChange()
{
    var baseline = LoadBaseline("IClaimProcessor.v2.1.json");
    var current = ExtractContract(typeof(IClaimProcessor));

    var detector = new ContractChangeDetector();
    var changes = detector.DetectChanges(baseline, current);

    Assert.True(changes.HasBreakingChanges);
    Assert.Contains(changes.BreakingChanges,
        c => c.Contains("ProcessAsync parameter count changed"));
}
```

**Impact**:
- All extensions implementing IClaimProcessor will fail to compile against v2.2
- CI/CD pipeline blocks deployment
- Extensions must be updated before product release

**Resolution**:
1. Notify all extension owners of breaking change
2. Provide migration guide
3. Optionally provide adapter class for backward compatibility:
   ```csharp
   public abstract class ClaimProcessorAdapter : IClaimProcessor
   {
       // New v2.2 method
       public abstract Task<ClaimResult> ProcessAsync(
           Claim claim, ProcessingContext context, CancellationToken cancellationToken);

       // Deprecated v2.1 method with default context
       [Obsolete("Use ProcessAsync with context parameter")]
       public Task<ClaimResult> ProcessAsync(Claim claim, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
       {
           return ProcessAsync(claim, ProcessingContext.Default, cancellationToken);
       }
   }
   ```

